INSERT INTO `wp_3_posts` VALUES (4,1,'2015-07-11 01:22:11','2015-07-11 00:22:11','Section to be expanded.','Math Basics','','publish','open','open','','math','','','2015-07-11 12:10:00','2015-07-11 06:40:00','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=1',0,'post','',0),(1,1,'2015-07-11 01:22:11','2015-07-10 19:52:11','Welcome to Learn YM, a project of yieldmore.org for learning subjects like math, science, history etc. Our purpose is to bring teachers and students together and provide a platform for our teachers to spread their learning. We are in the process of building our own content, but link to other online content when possible.\r\n\r\nOur teachers section is for content targeting teachers (by teacher trainers). And we also disseminate information for parents. We hope to build a network of teachers of schools and colleges and engage in collaboration, quality improvements in the education system and sharing resources with the needy.\r\n\r\nWe are based in Chennai, India and are focusing our efforts there for the moment, but if you are interested in facilitating/coordinating similar activity in any city in the world, please do not hesitate to contact us.\r\n\r\nFeel free to contact us by sending a mail to <a href=\"mailto:shasa@cselian.com?subject=YieldMore Learn Contact\" target=\"_blank\">shasa@cselian.com</a> with a slight background and how you can contribute / engage with us. You can also join the <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/groups/LearnYM/\" target=\"_blank\">Facebook Group</a>.\r\n\r\nIn time we also wish to provide the tools for teachers to conduct classes online, manage libraries esp for government school students, distribute pamphlets etc.','About','','publish','open','closed','','about','','','2015-08-24 21:17:17','2015-08-24 15:47:17','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?page_id=2',1,'page','',0),(3,1,'2015-08-17 18:08:50','2015-08-17 12:38:50','<img src=\"http://www.actionforhappiness.org/media/299704/values_cloud_2_599x225.jpg\" alt=\"values\" />\r\n\r\nValues are needed for all ages and all cultures. Â \r\nIn fact, culture imbibes values silently and traditions reinforce them.\r\n\r\nThe strong values serve as roots to keep the person do the right thing at the right time with right perspective agreeable to all.\r\nIn this section,  personal value (an individual\'s absolute or relative), and ethical value (the assumption of which can be the basis for ethical action) will be discussed and a set of consistent values and measures will be taught to children through playful activities on which they will reflect even after the session is over.\r\n\r\nFollowing is a set of 50 exercises developed by Prof PJ Naidu which he has graciously allowed us to use. See the individual articles in the menu / download the whole document <a href=\"http://learn.yieldmore.org/wp-content/data3/docs/FiftyValues-LYM.doc\">here</a>.\r\n\r\n[work config fol=students/values][/work]\r\n','Values','','publish','open','open','','values','','','2016-01-31 15:02:43','2016-01-31 09:32:43','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=5',0,'post','',1),(5,1,'2015-08-24 22:40:50','2015-08-24 17:10:50','[info]\r\nwiki: Sex education\r\nlink: http://sexetc.org/\r\nlink: http://parentingteens.about.com/od/teensexuality/a/sex_education.htm\r\n[/info]\r\n\r\n[snippet id=stub]\r\nIn this article, we will guide you to resources you can point your children to, give you tips on how/when to broach the subject and what are the special cases and extra support that your child may need.\r\n\r\nWe will also discuss abuse and preventive / responsive measures.','Sex Education','','publish','open','open','','sex-education','','','2015-08-24 22:44:13','2015-08-24 17:14:13','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=11',0,'post','',0),(6,1,'2015-09-12 14:47:48','2015-09-12 09:17:48','<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1GOnQvpeItplIX4RnFxQwUGD3wcxXC-ojiOTlhUW4NK4/\" target=\"_blank\">View the Source Google Spreadsheet</a>\r\n\r\n[faqs source=docs/faqs-parent.tsv][/faqs]\r\n','Parent FAQs','','publish','open','open','','faqs-parent','','','2015-09-12 14:48:44','2015-09-12 09:18:44','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=8',0,'post','',0),(7,5,'2015-09-15 21:32:26','2015-09-15 16:02:26','A vast subject, you can refer to these links for further information\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://jsswindia.com/issues/2015/2/06-writing-articles-for-research-journals\" target=\"_blank\">Writing Articles for Research Journals</a>\r\n\r\nA presentation made by Messrs PJ Naidu and Imran at the Madras University can be <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Y5QOE-QDxbDjSyczh-Il_cUxfguB6z5XDO51vIRj3WQ/edit\" target=\"_blank\">seen here</a>.','Technical Writing','','publish','open','open','','technical-writing','','','2015-09-15 21:38:52','2015-09-15 16:08:52','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=9',0,'post','',0),(9,5,'2015-10-26 09:51:38','2015-10-26 04:21:38','Artists technicians and administrators are to be found in this line of work\r\n\r\nFor now we have the support ofÂ <a href=\"http://greenroomnow.com\" target=\"_blank\">greenroomnow.com</a>, who will expand this section.','Film and Advertising','','publish','open','open','','film-and-ad','','','2015-10-30 14:25:35','2015-10-30 08:55:35','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=31',0,'post','',0),(8,2,'2016-01-27 13:07:32','2016-01-27 07:37:32','//TODO: Make a 2 page summary and move to a sub-page\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/13VI5TEYa_PgKutxJ_j50gjyyhBZk6ryCuCYqlqydquY/embed?start=true&amp;loop=true&amp;delayms=5000\" target=\"_blank\">5 \'S\'es in the workplace (Sort / Systematize / Sweep, Shine, Scrub / Standardize / Self-Discipline)</a> by NS Hariharan</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n','General','','publish','open','open','','general','','','2016-01-27 13:22:32','2016-01-27 07:52:32','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=35',0,'post','',0),(11,1,'2016-10-15 20:44:46','2016-10-15 15:14:46','Dear Patrons,\r\n\r\nGreetings from CHILD &amp; Blink Research and Services!\r\n\r\nLearning Disability (Dyslexia) is often termed as a hidden handicap, though to affect around 10-12% of the population. Some dyslexic adults have literacy problems and their problems exists or continues to tag along with them like erratic spelling, difficulty in comprehending written material, difficulty to scan or skim text etc. The positive side is their â€˜hidden potentialâ€™- of that there is a range of skills which include big picture thinking, problem solving skills, lateral thinking, originality, creativity and an instinctive understanding of how things work etc. Famous individuals with Learning Disabilities include Albert Einstein, Agatha Christie, Tom Cruise, artists, architects, engineers, entrepreneurs, and sportsmen. Not all with dyslexia will have outstanding talents but all will have comparative strengths and a determination to achieve.\r\n\r\nBlink Research and Services (BRS) is a start-up attempting to promote child development strategies and practices that will help reach out to all children, not leaving behind any. Thus its interest in attending to children with learning difficulties. In order to complement the focus on these children, BRS has partnered with a reputed institution â€“ CHILD (Centre for Holistic Integrated Learning and Development). CHILD was established to aid the lives of such children with a mission <strong>â€˜No child is left behindâ€™</strong>. It is in this context, CHILD along with Blink Research and Services (BRS) is conducting <strong>Trikaâ€™16, an International Conference, first time in India, on the theme â€“â€œEmpowering to Empowerâ€ - <em>Empowering Adults with Dyslexia towards Sustainable Development</em></strong>. The focus is on the topics that will help students take the transition from college to employment smoothly. The conference is scheduled on Saturday, 17th December 2016 in Loyola College, from 8:30 am to 5 pm.\r\n\r\nWe would like to invite you to become a sponsor for this noble cause, as the generosity of individuals like you makes events like these successful. A high level of experts in this field will share their expertise to the delegates. We are expecting a delegate registration of 300 people. We are also bringing out a souvenir that will be distributed to the participants at the conference. We invite you to be a partner with us in this event. <a href=\"/wp-content/data3/conferences/trika16/docs/trika16-sponsorship-letter.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">In this document</a> are options designed to assist you in finding the right level for your budget and your time. Please review and let us know.\r\n\r\nThank you in advance for your valuable time. \r\n\r\nJayashree Ashok,\r\nFounder, CHILD\r\n+91 98408 15265\r\n<a href=\"mailto:childincorp@gmail.com\" target=\"_blank\">childincorp@gmail.com</a>\r\n\r\nArun Fernandez,\r\nManaging Director, BRS\r\n\r\n[work config slim fol=conferences/trika16 logo=child-brs.jpg][/work]','Trika 2016','','publish','open','open','','trika16','','','2016-12-01 22:06:44','2016-12-01 16:36:44','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=11',0,'post','',0),(12,1,'2016-10-17 11:26:27','2016-10-17 05:56:27','Dear Patrons,\n\nGreetings form CHILD &amp; Blink Research and Services!\n\nLearning Disability (Dyslexia) is often termed as a hidden handicap, though to affect around 10-12% of the population. Some dyslexic adults have literacy problems and their problems exists or continues to tag along with them like erratic spelling, difficulty in comprehending written material, difficulty to scan or skim text etc. The positive side is their â€˜hidden potentialâ€™- of that there is a range of skills which include big picture thinking, problem solving skills, lateral thinking, originality, creativity and an instinctive understanding of how things work etc. Famous individuals with Learning Disabilities include Albert Einstein, Agatha Christie, Tom Cruise, artists, architects, engineers, entrepreneurs, and sportsmen. Not all with dyslexia will have outstanding talents but all will have comparative strengths and a determination to achieve.\n\nBlink Research and Services (BRS) is a start-up attempting to promote child development strategies and practices that will help reach out to all children, not leaving behind any. Thus its interest in attending to children with learning difficulties. In order to complement the focus on these children, BRS has partnered with a reputed institution â€“ CHILD (Centre for Holistic Integrated Learning and Development). CHILD was established to aid the lives of such children with a mission <strong>â€˜No child is left behindâ€™</strong>. It is in this context, CHILD along with Blink Research and Services (BRS) is conducting <strong>Trikaâ€™16, an International Conference, first time in India, on the theme â€“â€œEmpowering to Empowerâ€ - <em>Empowering Adults with Dyslexia towards Sustainable Development</em></strong>. The focus is on the topics that will help students take the transition from college to employment smoothly. The conference is scheduled on Saturday, 17th December 2016 in Loyola College, from 8:30 am to 5 pm.\n\nWe would like to invite you to become a sponsor for this noble cause, as the generosity of individuals like you makes events like these successful. A high level of experts in this field will share their expertise to the delegates. We are expecting a delegate registration of 300 people. We are also bringing out a souvenir that will be distributed to the participants at the conference. We invite you to be a partner with us in this event. <a href=\"/wp-content/data3/conferences/trika16/docs/trika16-sponsorship-letter.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">In this document</a> are options designed to assist you in finding the right level for your budget and your time. Please review and let us know.\n\nThank you in advance for your valuable time. \n\nJayashree Ashok,\nFounder, CHILD\n+91 98408 15265\n<a href=\"mailto:childincorp@gmail.com\" target=\"_blank\">childincorp@gmail.com</a>\n\nArun Fernandez,\nManaging Director, BRS\n\n[work config slim fol=conferences/trika16 logo=child-brs.jpg][/work]','Trika 2016','','inherit','closed','closed','','11-autosave-v1','','','2016-10-17 11:26:27','2016-10-17 05:56:27','',11,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/uncategorised/11-autosave-v1/',0,'revision','',0),(13,2,'2017-02-01 20:30:58','2017-02-01 15:00:58','<strong><a href=\"http://sinchanafoundation.org\" target=\"_blank\">Sinchana Foundation</a></strong>, established in July 2014, is a centre for individuals / children with special needs. It is located in one of the prime areas of Bangalore. The foundation is aimed towards working with the physically and the mentally challenged individuals who may be under the umbrella of Autism Spectrum Disorder, Cerebral Palsy, Learning Disability, Dyslexia, mental retardation, Downâ€™s Syndrome, Slow learners etc\r\n\r\nSinchana Foundation was formulated with a vision to create opportunities for individuals with special needs, to provide them a platform to tap their innate potentials.\r\n\r\nWe have more than a dozen children currently and conduct one-on-one, therapy classes. We have recently started occupational therapy sessions for those children with the need.\r\nâ€‹\r\n<strong>We are committed</strong> to work with these children, and provide them ample support using the latest technology and various updated educational  inputs. We hope to make a difference, and bring them into mainstream,  though in a small way, in the childâ€™s life.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<strong>Future Plans</strong>:\r\nâ€‹We would like to start  a school curriculum (10 am to 3 pm) for these children, especially who would not get admission in any of the other normal schools, using the Montessori methodology. This would be only the third and a one of a kind institute in Bangalore.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n','Sinchana Foundation','','publish','closed','open','','sinchana','','','2017-02-01 20:33:53','2017-02-01 15:03:53','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=13',0,'post','',0),(14,2,'2017-02-01 20:32:35','2017-02-01 15:02:35','[info]\n<strong>Sinchana Foundation</strong>, established in July 2014, is a centre for individuals / children with special needs. It is located in one of the prime areas of Bangalore. The foundation is aimed towards working with the physically and the mentally challenged individuals who may be under the umbrella of Autism Spectrum Disorder, Cerebral Palsy, Learning Disability, Dyslexia, mental retardation, Downâ€™s Syndrome, Slow learners etc\n\nSinchana Foundation was formulated with a vision to create opportunities for individuals with special needs, to provide them a platform to tap their innate potentials.\n\nWe have more than a dozen children currently and conduct one-on-one, therapy classes. We have recently started occupational therapy sessions for those children with the need.\nâ€‹\n<strong>We are committed</strong> to work with these children, and provide them ample support using the latest technology and various updated educational  inputs. We hope to make a difference, and bring them into mainstream,  though in a small way, in the childâ€™s life.\n\n\n\n<blockquote>\n<strong>Future Plans</strong>:\nâ€‹We would like to start  a school curriculum (10 am to 3 pm) for these children, especially who would not get admission in any of the other normal schools, using the Montessori methodology. This would be only the third and a one of a kind institute in Bangalore.\n</blockquote>\n','Sinchana Foundation','','inherit','closed','closed','','13-autosave-v1','','','2017-02-01 20:32:35','2017-02-01 15:02:35','',13,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/uncategorised/13-autosave-v1/',0,'revision','',0),(17,2,'2017-04-30 00:49:45','2017-04-29 19:19:45','[info]\r\nsource: https://www.thebalance.com/team-norms-sample-1919230\r\n[/info]\r\n\r\nEvery team has two components that team members must pay attention to if the team is going to succeed. The team must pay attention to the content mission (or goals or outcomes) expected from the team.\r\n\r\nThis is the content Â that the organization has asked the team to create or the <a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/mission-is-what-you-do-1918182\">mission that is the reason for the team existing</a> in the first place.\r\n\r\nThe team must also carefully shape and monitor the <a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/tips-for-better-teamwork-1919225\">team process it uses</a> to<a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/how-to-set-and-achieve-goals-1918137\">accomplish the goals</a>.\r\n\r\n<p>Team members must get along, respect each other, and practice effective interpersonal relationship building.</p>\r\nTeam process includes:\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>How the team members interact with and communicate with each other,</li>\r\n 	<li>How the <a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/tips-for-team-building-1918512\">team members communicate</a> with employees who are not on the team, and</li>\r\n 	<li>How team members will be responsible and accountable for moving the project forward and accomplishing the goals.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\nLong cited statistics demonstrate clearly where the majority of teams experience their most significant problems. They attribute 80% of the problems they experience to the process side of this equation. Teams experience 20% of their problems on the content or mission part of the equation.\r\n\r\nThis explains why the development of team norms for the process side of the equation is so important. Norms will naturally become established as people work together on the project. Why not create norms that support the accomplishment of the team\'s goals - sooner and with conscious consideration.\r\n\r\n<h3>See the Sample Team Norms</h3>\r\nThese team norms or ground rules are established with all members of the team participating equally. The manager of the team or the team\'s company sponsor or champion is included in the discussion and must agree to practice the relationship guidelines developed.\r\n\r\nIn prior articles, I discussed:\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/how-and-why-to-create-team-norms-1919229\">How and Why to Create Team Norms</a> and</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/how-to-develop-group-norms-step-by-step-1919228\">How to Develop Group Norms: Step by Step to Adopt Group Guidelines</a>.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\nHere are sample process norms or guidelines that a team might use to effectively conduct its business. You may use them as a starting point, but each team needs to go through the process of generating and committing to its own team norms. The team must <strong>own</strong> the norms.\r\n\r\n<h3>Sample Team Norms or Guidelines</h3>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>Treat each other with dignity and respect.</li>\r\n 	<li>Transparency: avoid hidden agendas.</li>\r\n 	<li>Be genuine with each other about ideas, challenges, and feelings.</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/trust-rules-the-most-important-secret-about-trust-1919393\">Trust each other</a>. Have confidence that issues discussed will be kept in confidence.</li>\r\n 	<li>Managers will open up a space in which people have information and are comfortable asking for what they need.</li>\r\n 	<li>Team members will practice a consistent commitment to sharing all the information they have. Share the complete information that you have up front.</li>\r\n 	<li><a href=\"https://www.thebalance.com/want-to-become-a-better-listener-1918650\">Listen first to understand</a>, and donâ€™t be dismissive of the input received when we listen.</li>\r\n 	<li>Practice being open-minded.</li>\r\n 	<li>Donâ€™t be defensive with your colleagues.</li>\r\n 	<li>Rather than searching for the guilty, give your colleagues the benefit of the doubt; have a clean slate process.</li>\r\n 	<li>Support each other - don\'t throw each other under the bus.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>Avoid territoriality; think instead of the overall good for the company, our employees, and our customers.</li>\r\n 	<li>The discussion of issues, ideas and direction will not become a personal attack or return to haunt you in the future.</li>\r\n 	<li>Managers are open, communicative, and authentic with each other and their teams.</li>\r\n 	<li>It\'s okay to not know the right answerÂ and to admit it. The team can find the answer.</li>\r\n 	<li>Problems are presented in a way that promotes mutual discussion and resolution.</li>\r\n 	<li>It is safe to be wrong as a manager. Thoughtful decision-making is expected.</li>\r\n 	<li>Own the whole implementation of the product, not just your little piece; recognize that you are part of something larger than yourself. Be responsible to own the whole picture.</li>\r\n 	<li>Practice and experience humility - each of us may not have all the answers.</li>\r\n 	<li>If you commit to doing something â€“ do it. Be accountable and responsible for the team.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>It is okay to be the messenger with bad news. You can expect a problem-solving approach, not recrimination.</li>\r\n 	<li>Promise to come prepared to your meetings and projects so that you demonstrate value and respect for the time and convenience of others.</li>\r\n 	<li>Strive to continuously improve and achieve the team\'s strategic goals. Don\'t let ineffective relationships and interactions sabotage the team\'s work.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\nTeams need to expend the effort to practice all of these norms and to care enough about the team and its work to confront each other, with care, compassion, and purpose, when a team member fails to practice these norms.\r\n','Group Norms or Relationship Guidelines','','publish','closed','open','','team','','','2017-04-30 00:50:18','2017-04-29 19:20:18','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=17',0,'post','',0),(18,2,'2017-08-27 06:42:07','2017-08-27 01:12:07','Jobs for Dyslexics, USA (<a href=\"http://jobsfordyslexics.org\" target=\"_blank\">jobsfordyslexics.org</a>) was begun in 2013 by Laura Cianci, with the intention of guiding organizations to build an enabling atmosphere where its dyslexic workers could be made more productive, by education and training.\r\n\r\nWe believe in a nurturing work environment where each person contributes the best of their skills and lead happy, fulfilling and contented lives. Itâ€™s only when individuals are personally happy, and inspired that they put in their best efforts. It is our mission to remove any obstacles that come in the way of this, esp rendering assistance to dyslexic persons by helping them augment their skills to better cope with their differing abilities. We do this by educating their companies on different work ethics and guidelines to get the best of their employees, one and all.\r\n\r\n[work config fol=orgs/jfd][/work]','Jobs for Dyslexics','','publish','closed','open','','jobs-for-dyslexics','','','2017-08-27 06:48:04','2017-08-27 01:18:04','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=18',0,'post','',0),(19,1,'2017-10-27 23:37:52','0000-00-00 00:00:00','I thought of adding this page when my mother was asked to teach painting to some children.\r\n\r\nA <a href=\"https://www.google.co.in/search?q=how+to+teach+painting+to+beginners\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">quick Google</a> showed these links:','Painting Sketching Drawing et. al.','','draft','closed','open','','painting','','','2017-10-27 23:37:52','2017-10-27 18:07:52','',0,'http://learn.yieldmore.org/?p=19',0,'post','',0),(21,1,'2018-10-13 12:16:46','2018-10-13 06:46:46','Sanskrit , a magical language\r\n\r\n1. There is no need of particular sentence structure for Sanskrit.\r\nLike, In English:-Â Subject +Verb + Object\r\nEx:- I am writing an answer.\r\n\r\nBut in Sanskrit there is no need for particular structure.Â \r\nà¤…à¤¹à¤‚ à¤‰à¤¤à¥à¤¤à¤°à¤®à¥Â à¤²à¤¿à¤–à¤¾à¤®à¤¿ (I am writing an Answer.)\r\nà¤²à¤¿à¤–à¤¾à¤®à¤¿ à¤…à¤¹à¤‚ à¤‰à¤¤à¥à¤¤à¤°à¤®à¥ (I am writing an Answer.)\r\nà¤…à¤¹à¤‚ à¤²à¤¿à¤–à¤¾à¤®à¤¿ à¤‰à¤¤à¥à¤¤à¤°à¤®à¥ (I am writing an Answer.) .Â \r\n\r\n2. Elephant word has 4000 synonyms in Sanskrit. Here are some of them.\r\nà¤•à¥à¤žà¥à¤œà¤°à¤ƒ,à¤—à¤œà¤ƒ,à¤¹à¤¸à¥à¤¤à¤¿à¤¨à¥, à¤¹à¤¸à¥à¤¤à¤¿à¤ªà¤•à¤ƒ,à¤¦à¥à¤µà¤¿à¤ªà¤ƒ,à¤¦à¥à¤µà¤¿à¤°à¤¦à¤ƒ,à¤µà¤¾à¤°à¤£à¤ƒ,à¤•à¤°à¤¿à¤¨à¥,à¤®à¤¤à¤™à¥à¤—à¤ƒ,à¤¸à¥à¤šà¤¿à¤•à¤¾à¤§à¤°à¤ƒ, à¤¸à¥à¤ªà¥à¤°à¤¤à¥€à¤•à¤ƒ, à¤…à¤™à¥à¤—à¥‚à¤·à¤ƒ, à¤…à¤¨à¥à¤¤à¥‡à¤ƒà¤¸à¥à¤µà¥‡à¤¦à¤ƒ, à¤‡à¤­à¤ƒ, à¤•à¤žà¥à¤œà¤°à¤ƒ, à¤•à¤žà¥à¤œà¤¾à¤°à¤ƒ, à¤•à¤Ÿà¤¿à¤¨à¥, à¤•à¤®à¥à¤¬à¥à¤ƒ, à¤•à¤°à¤¿à¤•à¤ƒ, à¤•à¤¾à¤²à¤¿à¤™à¥à¤—à¤ƒ, à¤•à¥‚à¤šà¤ƒ, à¤—à¤°à¥à¤œà¤ƒ, à¤šà¤¦à¤¿à¤°à¤ƒ, à¤šà¤•à¥à¤°à¤ªà¤¾à¤¦à¤ƒ, à¤šà¤¨à¥à¤¦à¤¿à¤°à¤ƒ, à¤œà¤²à¤•à¤¾à¤™à¥à¤•à¥à¤·à¤ƒ, à¤œà¤°à¥à¤¤à¥à¤ƒ, à¤¦à¤£à¥à¤¡à¤µà¤²à¤§à¤¿à¤ƒ, à¤¦à¤¨à¥à¤¤à¤¾à¤µà¤²à¤ƒ, à¤¦à¥€à¤°à¥à¤˜à¤ªà¤µà¤¨à¤ƒ, à¤¦à¥€à¤°à¥à¤˜à¤µà¤•à¥à¤¤à¥à¤°à¤ƒ, à¤¦à¥à¤°à¥à¤®à¤¾à¤°à¤¿à¤ƒ, à¤¦à¥à¤µà¤¿à¤¦à¤¨à¥à¤¤à¤ƒ, à¤¦à¥à¤µà¤¿à¤°à¤¾à¤ªà¤ƒ, à¤¨à¤—à¤œà¤ƒ, à¤¨à¤—à¤°à¤˜à¤¾à¤¤à¤ƒ, à¤¨à¤°à¥à¤¤à¤•à¤ƒ, à¤¨à¤¿à¤°à¥à¤à¤°à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¤žà¥à¤šà¤¨à¤–à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¤¿à¤šà¤¿à¤²à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¥€à¤²à¥à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¤¿à¤£à¥à¤¡à¤ªà¤¾à¤¦à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¤¿à¤£à¥à¤¡à¤ªà¤¾à¤¦à¥à¤¯à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¥ƒà¤¦à¤¾à¤•à¥à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¥ƒà¤·à¥à¤Ÿà¤¹à¤¾à¤¯à¤¨à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¥à¤£à¥à¤¡à¥à¤°à¤•à¥‡à¤²à¤¿à¤ƒ, à¤¬à¥ƒà¤¹à¤¦à¤™à¥à¤—à¤ƒ, à¤ªà¥à¤°à¤¸à¥à¤µà¥‡à¤¦à¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¦à¤•à¤²à¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¦à¤¾à¤°à¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¹à¤¾à¤•à¤¾à¤¯à¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¹à¤¾à¤®à¥ƒà¤—à¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¹à¤¾à¤¨à¤¾à¤¦à¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¾à¤¤à¤‚à¤—à¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¤à¤‚à¤—à¤œà¤ƒ, à¤®à¤¤à¥à¤¤à¤•à¥€à¤¶à¤ƒ, à¤°à¤¾à¤œà¤¿à¤²à¤ƒ, à¤°à¤¾à¤œà¥€à¤µà¤ƒ, à¤°à¤•à¥à¤¤à¤ªà¤¾à¤¦à¤ƒ, à¤°à¤£à¤®à¤¤à¥à¤¤à¤ƒ, à¤°à¤¸à¤¿à¤•à¤ƒ, à¤²à¤®à¥à¤¬à¤•à¤°à¥à¤£à¤ƒ, à¤²à¤¤à¤¾à¤²à¤•à¤ƒ, à¤²à¤¤à¤¾à¤°à¤¦à¤ƒ, à¤µà¤¨à¤œà¤ƒ, à¤µà¤°à¤¾à¤™à¥à¤—à¤ƒ, à¤µà¤¾à¤°à¥€à¤Ÿà¤ƒ, à¤µà¤¿à¤¤à¤£à¥à¤¡à¤ƒ, à¤·à¤·à¥à¤Ÿà¤¿à¤¹à¤¾à¤¯à¤¨à¤ƒ, à¤µà¥‡à¤¦à¤£à¥à¤¡à¤ƒ, à¤µà¥‡à¤—à¤¦à¤£à¥à¤¡à¤ƒ, à¤µà¥‡à¤¤à¤£à¥à¤¡à¤ƒ, à¤µà¤¿à¤²à¥‹à¤®à¤œà¤¿à¤¹à¥à¤µà¤ƒ, à¤µà¤¿à¤²à¥‹à¤®à¤°à¤¸à¤¨à¤ƒ, à¤µà¤¿à¤·à¤¾à¤£à¤•à¤ƒà¥¤\r\n\r\nThis is just one example. Have you seen any language with such rich vocabulary?\r\n\r\n3. Magha was a great Sanskrit Poet and Author. He was an expert in writing a whole Sloka with one-two-three-four consonants. Here is just an example from his bookÂ Shishupala Vadha:-Â \r\n\r\nIn 144th stanza, he writes whole sloka with onlyÂ one consonant.\r\nà¤¦à¤¾à¤¦à¤¦à¥‹ à¤¦à¥à¤¦à¥à¤¦à¤¦à¥à¤¦à¥à¤¦à¤¾à¤¦à¥€ à¤¦à¤¾à¤¦à¤¦à¥‹ à¤¦à¥‚à¤¦à¤¦à¥€à¤¦à¤¦à¥‹à¤ƒ à¥¤Â \r\nà¤¦à¥à¤¦à¥à¤¦à¤¾à¤¦à¤‚ à¤¦à¤¦à¤¦à¥‡ à¤¦à¥à¤¦à¥à¤¦à¥‡ à¤¦à¤¾à¤¦à¤¾à¤¦à¤¦à¤¦à¤¦à¥‹à¤½à¤¦à¤¦à¤ƒ à¥¥\r\n\r\n(Translation:- Sri Krishna, the giver of every boon, the scourge of the evil-minded, the purifier, the one whose arms can annihilate the wicked who cause suffering to others, shot his pain-causing arrow at the enemy.)\r\n\r\nAlso, he was an expert in writingÂ palindromes. He writes in 44th stanza:-à¤µà¤¾à¤°à¤£à¤¾à¤—à¤—à¤­à¥€à¤°à¤¾ à¤¸à¤¾ à¤¸à¤¾à¤°à¤¾à¤­à¥€à¤—à¤—à¤£à¤¾à¤°à¤µà¤¾ à¥¤Â \r\nà¤•à¤¾à¤°à¤¿à¤¤à¤¾à¤°à¤¿à¤µà¤§à¤¾ à¤¸à¥‡à¤¨à¤¾ à¤¨à¤¾à¤¸à¥‡à¤§à¤¾ à¤µà¤¾à¤°à¤¿à¤¤à¤¾à¤°à¤¿à¤•à¤¾ à¥¥\r\n\r\n(Translation:- It is very difficult to face this army which is endowed with elephants as big as mountains. This is a very great army and the shouting of frightened people is heard. It has slain its enemies.)\r\n\r\n4. Have you heard about any book which can give you different story when you read it from backward? Here is a bookÂ Sri Raghava Yadhaveeyam.Â \r\n\r\nThis book is written in such a way that you will enjoy the story ofÂ RamaÂ when you read it in forward way while you will enjoy the story ofÂ KrishnaÂ when you read it from backward.\r\n\r\nForward:-Â \r\nà¤µà¤¨à¥à¤¦à¥‡à¤½à¤¹à¤‚ à¤¦à¥‡à¤µà¤‚ à¤¤à¤‚ à¤¶à¥à¤°à¥€à¤¤à¤‚ à¤°à¤¨à¥à¤¤à¤¾à¤°à¤‚ à¤•à¤¾à¤²à¤‚ à¤­à¤¾à¤¸à¤¾ à¤¯à¤ƒ à¥¤\r\nà¤°à¤¾à¤®à¥‹ à¤°à¤¾à¤®à¤¾à¤§à¥€à¤°à¤¾à¤ªà¥à¤¯à¤¾à¤—à¥‹ à¤²à¥€à¤²à¤¾à¤®à¤¾à¤°à¤¾à¤¯à¥‹à¤§à¥à¤¯à¥‡ à¤µà¤¾à¤¸à¥‡ à¥¥\r\n\r\n(Translation:- I pay my obeisance toÂ Lord Shri Rama, who with his heart pining for Sita, travelled across theÂ Sahyadri HillsÂ and returned to Ayodhya after killingÂ RavanaÂ and sported with his consort, Sita, in Ayodhya for a long time.)\r\n\r\nBackward:-\r\nà¤¸à¥‡à¤µà¤¾à¤§à¥à¤¯à¥‡à¤¯à¥‹ à¤°à¤¾à¤®à¤¾à¤²à¤¾à¤²à¥€ à¤—à¥‹à¤ªà¥à¤¯à¤¾à¤°à¤¾à¤§à¥€ à¤®à¤¾à¤°à¤¾à¤®à¥‹à¤°à¤¾ à¥¤\r\nà¤¯à¤¸à¥à¤¸à¤¾à¤­à¤¾à¤²à¤‚à¤•à¤¾à¤°à¤‚ à¤¤à¤¾à¤°à¤‚ à¤¤à¤‚ à¤¶à¥à¤°à¥€à¤¤à¤‚ à¤µà¤¨à¥à¤¦à¥‡à¤¹à¤‚ à¤¦à¥‡à¤µà¤‚ à¥¥\r\n\r\n(Translation:- I bow toÂ Lord Shri Krishna, whose chest is the sporting resort ofÂ Shri Lakshmi; who is fit to be contemplated through penance and sacrifice, who fondlesÂ RukmaniÂ and his other consorts and who is worshipped by the gopis, and who is decked with jewels radiating splendour.)\r\n\r\n5. NASA \r\nscientistÂ Rick BriggsÂ said that Sanskrit is the only unambiguous language in existence. He also wrote some explanations about Sanskrit in his article.\r\n\r\n6. Sanskrit is a language which is used asÂ Speech Therapy.Â Sanskrit has five different classes of word â€”Â KanthyaÂ (Spoken from throat),Â TalavyaÂ (Spoken while touching tongue to jaw),Â DantyaÂ (Spoken while touching tongue to teeth),Â MurdhanyaÂ (Spoken by twisting tongue),Â OstyaÂ (Spoken by lips).Â \r\n\r\n7. Sanskrit has been identified as the most suitable programming languages for computers to understand. Research is going on to make a programming language in Sanskrit which can be compiled and executed million times faster than other programming languages.','Sankrit','','publish','closed','open','','sankrit','','','2018-10-13 12:16:46','2018-10-13 06:46:46','',0,'http://yieldmore.org/learn/?p=21',0,'post','',0),(22,1,'2018-11-07 12:44:52','2018-11-07 07:14:52','[info]\r\nsource: https://byrdword.com/\r\nCurated: US\r\n[/info]\r\n\r\nHow do we stay focused in this \"age of distraction\"? \r\n\r\nThe reality is that we are, and always will be, exposed to distraction whether at home or the office. \r\n\r\nMany of us are connected to our technology all day, which in some cases increases our inability to focus and complete our daily tasks.\r\n\r\nIt becomes more important by the day to learn how to avoid the distractions of this over-stimulating world. \r\n\r\nCheck out this mindmap with strategies and tips to stay focused in the \"age of distraction\"...\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://yieldmore.org/learn/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2018/11/byrdword-how-to-focus.jpg\"><img src=\"http://yieldmore.org/learn/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2018/11/byrdword-how-to-focus-300x218.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"218\" class=\"aligncenter size-medium wp-image-23\" /></a>\r\n\r\nInfographic Created by Learning Fundamentals \r\n\r\nThe ability to focus is an important factor for success... Create good habits, manage your workspace and e-mail, detox from technology (disconnect), work in focused bursts, and take time to reflect!\r\n\r\nSpend some time reflecting on this mindmap and pull out some tricks that will motivate you to eliminate distractions in your own life. ðŸ™‚ \r\n\r\nCheers to a productive weekend!','How to Focus','','publish','closed','open','','how-to-focus','','','2018-11-07 12:44:52','2018-11-07 07:14:52','',0,'http://yieldmore.org/learn/?p=22',0,'post','',0),(23,1,'2018-11-07 12:43:59','2018-11-07 07:13:59','','byrdword-how-to-focus','','inherit','closed','closed','','byrdword-how-to-focus','','','2018-11-07 12:43:59','2018-11-07 07:13:59','',22,'http://yieldmore.org/learn/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2018/11/byrdword-how-to-focus.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(25,1,'2018-11-16 09:24:15','2018-11-16 03:54:15','Cartography is the science or practice of drawing maps. Has many allusions to the real world and how we perceive it.\r\n\r\nUntil this is expanded, pls have a look at this article by one of our long-term friends Shekar, owner of Street Atlas, from Fraser Town, Bangalore.\r\n\r\n<iframe style=\"width: 100%; height: 1000px;\" src=\"http://docs.google.com/gview?url=http://yieldmore.org/wp-content/data3/fields/cartography/docs/1808-shekar-bangalore.pdf&amp;embedded=true\" frameborder=\"0\"></iframe>','Cartography','','publish','closed','open','','cartography','','','2018-11-16 09:24:15','2018-11-16 03:54:15','',0,'http://yieldmore.org/learn/?p=25',0,'post','',0),(27,1,'2019-08-23 18:13:22','2019-08-23 12:43:22','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Learning to play the keyboard is the best way to sense</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Keyboard / Piano','','publish','closed','open','','keyboard-piano','','','2019-08-23 18:13:22','2019-08-23 12:43:22','',0,'http://yieldmore.org/learn/?p=27',0,'post','',0);
